#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:9
msgid "Compare particle diffusion models"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:11
msgid ""
"In this notebook we compare the different models for mass transport within "
"the electrode particles. For a full list of all the particle models, see the "
"`documentation <https://docs.pybamm.org/en/latest/source/api/models/"
"submodels/particle/index.html>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:13
msgid ""
"With the \"Fickian diffusion\" option a diffusion equation is solved within "
"the particle domain, with the boundary flux prescribed at the surface "
"related to the local current density. Alternatively, one can assume a "
"particular (polynomial) concentration profile within the particle (at "
"present, this can be uniform, quadratic, or quartic). The \"uniform "
"profile\" model assumes that the concentration inside the particle is "
"uniform in space (and therefore equal to the surface concentration through "
"the entire particle - in effect ignoring transport resistance within the "
"particle), and solves an ODE for the average particle concentration. The "
"\"quadratic profile\" model additionally solves an algebraic equation for "
"the surface concentration, taking into account the effect of diffusion "
"within the particle. Finally, the \"quartic profile\" model also solves for "
"the average concentration gradient (the integral of :math:`\\partial c/ "
"\\partial r`) in the particle, giving a higher-order approximation to the "
"concentration profile within the particle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:17
msgid ""
"As the exchange current density is a function of surface concentration, we "
"can see the effect the choice of particle model has on the voltage profile "
"arising from different overpotentials."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:28
msgid "First we import the packages we're going to use"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[1]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"%pip install \"pybamm[plot,cite]\" -q    # install PyBaMM if it is not "
"installed\n"
"import os\n"
"\n"
"import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n"
"\n"
"import pybamm\n"
"\n"
"os.chdir(pybamm.__path__[0] + \"/..\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:56
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:62
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:69
msgid "Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:80
msgid ""
"We then create a list of models with the different particle diffusion "
"submodels using the options functionality"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"particle_options = [\n"
"    \"Fickian diffusion\",\n"
"    \"uniform profile\",\n"
"    \"quadratic profile\",\n"
"    \"quartic profile\",\n"
"]\n"
"models = [\n"
"    pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN(options={\"particle\": opt}, name=opt)\n"
"    for opt in particle_options\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:110
msgid ""
"Next we set up simulations for each model with the current set as an input "
"function. We will change this later to observe the effect on the different "
"models."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"simulations = []\n"
"for model in models:\n"
"    param = model.default_parameter_values\n"
"    param[\"Current function [A]\"] = \"[input]\"\n"
"    simulations.append(pybamm.Simulation(model, parameter_values=param))"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[4]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "t_eval = [0, 3600]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:145
msgid ""
"First we run the simulations for each model with a current of 0.68A - this "
"corresponds to a C-rate of about 1 with the standard cell size configured in "
"the default parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"solutions_1C = []\n"
"for sim in simulations:\n"
"    sim.solve(t_eval, inputs={\"Current function [A]\": 0.68})\n"
"    solutions_1C.append(sim.solution)\n"
"    print(f\"Particle model: {sim.model.name}\")\n"
"    print(f\"Solve time: {sim.solution.solve_time}s\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:171
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Particle model: Fickian diffusion\n"
"Solve time: 18.081 mss\n"
"Particle model: uniform profile\n"
"Solve time: 5.459 mss\n"
"Particle model: quadratic profile\n"
"Solve time: 10.637 mss\n"
"Particle model: quartic profile\n"
"Solve time: 10.988 mss\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:184
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Particle model: Fickian diffusion\n"
"Solve time: 18.081 mss\n"
"Particle model: uniform profile\n"
"Solve time: 5.459 mss\n"
"Particle model: quadratic profile\n"
"Solve time: 10.637 mss\n"
"Particle model: quartic profile\n"
"Solve time: 10.988 mss\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:198
msgid ""
"Particle model: Fickian diffusion\n"
"Solve time: 18.081 mss\n"
"Particle model: uniform profile\n"
"Solve time: 5.459 mss\n"
"Particle model: quadratic profile\n"
"Solve time: 10.637 mss\n"
"Particle model: quartic profile\n"
"Solve time: 10.988 mss"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:216
msgid ""
"By not solving the diffusion problem in the particles explicitly, and "
"instead assuming a polynomial profile, we can speed up the simulation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[6]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"plt.figure(figsize=(15, 15))\n"
"style = [\"k\", \"r*\", \"b^\", \"g--\"]\n"
"for i in range(len(models)):\n"
"    plt.plot(\n"
"        solutions_1C[i][\"Time [s]\"].entries,\n"
"        solutions_1C[i][\"Voltage [V]\"].entries,\n"
"        style[i],\n"
"        label=particle_options[i],\n"
"    )\n"
"plt.legend()\n"
"plt.title(\"Model Comparison 1C\")\n"
"plt.xlabel(\"Time [s]\")\n"
"plt.ylabel(\"Voltage [V]\")\n"
"plt.grid()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:258
msgid ""
"We can see that the Fickian, quadratic and quartic profiles agree very well "
"and that the uniform profile over-predicts the cell voltage and capacity by "
"ignoring this transport resistance. The only significant difference between "
"the Fickian and quadratic models is on the first datapoint when transient "
"effects after the initial state may differ. Observe what happens next when "
"we increase the current."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[7]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"t_eval = [0, 1800]\n"
"solutions_2C = []\n"
"for sim in simulations:\n"
"    sim.solve(t_eval, inputs={\"Current function [A]\": 2 * 0.68})\n"
"    solutions_2C.append(sim.solution)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[8]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"plt.figure(figsize=(15, 15))\n"
"for i in range(len(models)):\n"
"    plt.plot(\n"
"        solutions_2C[i][\"Time [s]\"].entries,\n"
"        solutions_2C[i][\"Voltage [V]\"].entries,\n"
"        style[i],\n"
"        label=particle_options[i],\n"
"    )\n"
"plt.legend()\n"
"plt.title(\"Model Comparison 2C\")\n"
"plt.xlabel(\"Time [s]\")\n"
"plt.ylabel(\"Voltage [V]\")\n"
"plt.grid()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:313
msgid ""
"The quadratic model is still much better at approximating Fickian diffusion "
"and the relative error in the uniform model has increased. However, the "
"initial error in the quadratic model has grown slightly. Increasing current "
"even more will highlight the problem further. The quartic model is still "
"providing an excellent match to the Fickian diffusion profile."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[9]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"t_eval = [0, 360]\n"
"solutions_6C = []\n"
"for sim in simulations:\n"
"    sim.solve(t_eval, inputs={\"Current function [A]\": 6 * 0.68})\n"
"    solutions_6C.append(sim.solution)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid "[10]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"plt.figure(figsize=(15, 15))\n"
"for i in range(len(models)):\n"
"    plt.plot(\n"
"        solutions_6C[i][\"Time [s]\"].entries,\n"
"        solutions_6C[i][\"Voltage [V]\"].entries,\n"
"        style[i],\n"
"        label=particle_options[i],\n"
"    )\n"
"plt.legend()\n"
"plt.title(\"Model Comparison 6C\")\n"
"plt.xlabel(\"Time [s]\")\n"
"plt.ylabel(\"Voltage [V]\")\n"
"plt.grid()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/compare-particle-diffusion-models.ipynb:368
msgid ""
"Now the quadratic profile assumption begins to breakdown and that initial "
"error propagates much further into the discharge. Happily the quartic model "
"is higher-order and the match to the Fickian profile is still very good."
msgstr ""

